# Page Architecture and SEO

- Follow the existing `src/pages`, `src/components/<feature>`, `src/data`, and `src/hooks` structure before introducing a new pattern.
- Keep route pages focused on composition and page state; move static content and typed models to `src/data/<feature>.ts`.
- Split substantial sections into feature components. Move a component to `components/common` only when it has at least two real consumers.
- Reuse the shared pagination, headings, cards, and hooks when their behavior matches; extend them without breaking existing consumers.
- Keep TypeScript types explicit at data and component boundaries. Do not use `any` or duplicate the same data shape across files.
- Give every public route a unique Arabic title, description, canonical URL, Open Graph metadata, and appropriate JSON-LD through the shared SEO utility.
- Use semantic HTML, one clear `h1`, logical heading levels, descriptive image alt text, labeled controls, visible focus, and 44px minimum interactive targets.
- Design mobile-first for 320, 375, 768, 1024, and 1440px; prevent horizontal overflow and reserve image space to avoid layout shift.
- Add calm, purposeful hover and scroll motion using transform/opacity. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` and never hide essential content behind animation.
- Keep medical claims cautious and source factual health content from authoritative references. Mark draft/demo content clearly until medically reviewed.
- Optimize images, lazy-load below-the-fold media, and avoid adding a dependency when a small reusable component or hook is sufficient.
- Add or update tests for routing, primary interactions, empty states, pagination, and SEO; run lint, typecheck, tests, and build before handoff.
